			<div class="content" id="post-content-4773355">
									

<p>So I was interested how fast can SCP-018 actually go. I whipped out a Python simulation assuming that the ball is 6&#160;cm in diameter, and is made of solid rubber with a density of 1.2&#160;kg/dm^3. I also used 0.47 drag coefficient taken from Wikipedia. The coefficient of restitution was 200% assuming that's what the article means. The ball was dropped from 1 meter assuming a perfectly vertical bounce which gives it the highest possible speed it can achieve.</p>
<p>The results are not nearly as crazy as is thought. The ball reaches a max velocity of around 250&#160;km/h right after bouncing from the ground and reaches an altitude of about 120 meters. As far as the article goes it was written fairly realistic whether intentionally or not. The ball could very well be bouncing around for a few days before landing in a lake.</p>
<p>Speaking of the lake, water has almost 800 times the density of air and because it is incompressible it has a lot more drag for fast objects than if it was a gas as dense as water. Long story short, even at 250&#160;km/h the ball stops almost instantly.</p>
<p>On another hand, the containment procedure seems to be a bit over the top. It doesn't take a gel or molten plastic to stop it and it would make a lot more sense to fill the tank with water which would be cheaper, more practical, and a lot safer for the containment crew.</p>
<p>Also, sorry for replying to a very old comment but I saw that no one seemed to take the time to actually find out what would happen and how dangerous actually is this SCP.</p>
